    18	\begin{abstract}
    19	% BEGIN-EV2
    20	A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that for the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing, because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. A registered test compares it with a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, at matched budget and sanctions on six markets drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
    21	Four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3 under D2+H3 attacks: \site{abs_q1_est}); exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences are [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] with the frozen searches, [\site{abs_ml_dlo}, \site{abs_ml_dhi}] with the doubled searches, and [\site{abs_env_lo}, \site{abs_env_hi}] with each rule's strongest family, so the direction is fairly stable but the size, and with it the equivalence label, is fragile and not established beyond these markets; the second attack family repeated the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, newest-first targeting does most of the work: in R3's own comparison (with and without the priority window, under D2+H3), the window changes the attacker's gain by \site{abs_dp}, small next to its effect without newest-first targeting. Third, at the headline market the targeting also falls on an honest newcomer: R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of a replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, C$^*$ \site{abs_hon_cs_1} in the same bin, and the replacement's profit per round under R3 differs from that under C$^*$ by a fraction of \site{abs_hon_rel}. Fourth, under D2+H3, R3 lowers the attacker's gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; at the headline market, welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0} while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 (gain \site{abs_q3_w0g}).
    22	The claims hold for the prescribed searches and frozen references, the evaluated markets, fixed parameters, the searched families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities, so honest churn is untested; C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.
