   257	\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). R3's selected W1 policy has an estimated gain of exactly zero, and the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C$^*$ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option to replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against them. These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, has the probability-weighted mean of its components' gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing evaluation records. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_e_cs} for C$^*$ at non-negative estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3's mixture (\site{r_ph_e_r3_d}) meets the registered training constraint, and C$^*$'s does so at the smaller weight \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, with efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs} against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). Weights and companions were chosen on the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided \const{95} percent bound, so these rows show that the prescribed W1 search did not consider episode-level mixtures that reach lower empirical efficiency; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
