A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that for the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing, because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. A registered test compares it with a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, at matched budget and sanctions on six markets drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
Four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3 under D2+H3 attacks: \site{abs_q1_est}); exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences are [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] with the frozen searches, [\site{abs_ml_dlo}, \site{abs_ml_dhi}] with the doubled searches, and [\site{abs_env_lo}, \site{abs_env_hi}] with each rule's strongest family, so the direction is fairly stable but the size, and with it the equivalence label, is fragile and not established beyond these markets; the second attack family repeated the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, newest-first targeting does most of the work: in R3's own comparison (with and without the priority window, under D2+H3), the window changes the attacker's gain by \site{abs_dp}, small next to its effect without newest-first targeting. Third, at the headline market the targeting also falls on an honest newcomer: R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of a replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, C$^*$ \site{abs_hon_cs_1} in the same bin, and the replacement's profit per round under R3 differs from that under C$^*$ by a fraction of \site{abs_hon_rel}. Fourth, under D2+H3, R3 lowers the attacker's gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; at the headline market, welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0} while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 (gain \site{abs_q3_w0g}).
The claims hold for the prescribed searches and frozen references, the evaluated markets, fixed parameters, the searched families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities, so honest churn is untested; C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.
% END-EV2
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are expensive and run after the response is served.
FlashProve, for example, proves that a committed integer model produced a given token under greedy decoding; the provider generates proofs after the response is served, at about \const{70} to \const{95} times the cost of one forward pass~\cite[p.~18]{rinberg2026flashprove}, for a window of \const{1024} tokens per request, and the client checks each proof on a CPU~\cite[pp.~16--17]{rinberg2026flashprove}. Its Lean proofs cover the protocol; the equivalence of the executable prover and verifier to it is not proved~\cite[p.~17]{rinberg2026flashprove}. Such proofs detect a substituted computation; they do not prevent an incorrect response from being served. If the checking party funds such checks, a budget forces a sample, and the questions become which jobs to check and how to sanction a provider whose check fails.

We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identities are unavailable. The mechanism class excludes entry fees, identity certification, and collateral before receipts, and a sanctioned provider may re-enter at once under a fresh identity. The rules refund the escrow of a failed job and exclude the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. The population is a fixed set of providers, each with one active identity at a time; concurrent aliases, pre-aged reserve identities, and additional entrants (Sybil attacks~\cite{douceur2002sybil}) are outside the model.

\paragraph{Checking newcomers is an old idea.} Intensive verification until an identity has earned credibility is established: credibility-based spot-checking analyses rejoining under new identities~\cite{sarmenta2001ccgrid,sarmenta2001sabotage}; BOINC offers an optional adaptive-replication policy that replicates a host's jobs until the host passes a trust threshold of consecutive valid results~\cite{anderson2009diversity,boincwiki}; customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new importers for a period~\cite{walsh2003postrelease}. Checking can also depend on what a participant reports~\cite{zarkoob2020spot}; our admissible rules may read offers and claims, but we study targeting by identity age and verified history. The question we ask is narrower: under a hard audit budget, does R3's persistent age-ranked targeting do better or worse than targeting by verified history, when everything else is held equal?

\paragraph{The rule.} For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, immediate free replacement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to identity relabelling, so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}). R3 gives audit priority to the jobs of identities in their first $L$ rounds, auditing all of them when the round's quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established identities. Its priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/\const{2} \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits, $s$ being its served jobs (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a replacement receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer entrants, or a quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity's first offer.

\paragraph{Two registered tests.} Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments. The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition, stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test's comparison.

\paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm0.05$; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first, and the classification is established for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain, so given newest-first targeting the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) In a registered diagnostic, R3 keeps auditing an honest provider's replacement identity well after its window, at a measured cost in that provider's profit per round: the targeting that works against the attacker also burdens honest newcomers. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
% BEGIN-EV2
\paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3 (the two families are close to one test, since X7-SC mostly selected the embedded D2+H3 winner): R3 is not materially better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget, and the difference varies in sign across markets. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the \site{int_w1} of the registered W1 search at non-negative estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures' population profitability is not established. Q4: the B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on the other three, R3's estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.
% END-EV2
\paragraph{The factorial (second test).} The eight factorial rules and the comparators share B3's allocation and payment, refund-and-ban enforcement, no bond, the allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, and the fixed pair $\tau = \const{0.16}$, $L = \const{10}$, which the registered tuning of B3 and of R3 had each chosen. They differ in three parts: a priority phase P (served jobs of identities younger than $L$ first, newest first), newest-first residual targeting G ($\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual audits to the newest identities), and pacing S (R3's terminal reserve and even early quota, against B3's spread schedule). The rules $F_{PGS}$ include $F_{000} = $ B3 and $F_{111} = $ R3. B2 keeps its posted prices and serves only as a reference at the headline market.

\paragraph{Verified-history targeting (second test).} The comparator family adapts credibility-based spot-checking and BOINC's adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity's credibility $p_i$ is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch after each round's audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, $p_i = 0$ for the next round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise $p_i$ grows by the round's passes. A replacement starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr($K$) treats an identity as unproven while $p_i < K$ and audits unproven identities first, in ascending $p_i$; it then selects $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual jobs by ascending $p_i$ plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the rest uniformly. C-mem($K$, $M$) counts only the passes of the last $M$ rounds, so scrutiny returns to identities that are not audited. C-taper($K$) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by weighted sampling without replacement, with weight $K/(p_i + K)$ (C-taper) or $1$ if $p_i < 10$ and $1/p_i$ otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC's trust threshold of 10~\cite{boincwiki}); the weights are relative selection weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed pair and R3's pacing, by the development tuning objective: the minimum, over the headline market, a development market, and the attack families, of the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an attacker-gain margin. The efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration, so the selection was in effect on gross efficiency, and at the inner search budget every configuration's selected attack had zero or negative gain (Table~\ref{tab:comparator-dev}). The two best were rechecked at full budget; the selected comparator is C$^* = $ C-mem($K = \const{6}$, $M = \const{20}$), and the runner-up is C-thr($K = \const{12}$). Both full-budget scores were decided by D2 at dev0 (\const{0.103} against \const{0.098}), while under D2+H3 the runner-up left the lower gain (\const{0.076} against \const{0.097} at the headline market, \const{0.018} against \const{0.038} at dev0). This is a two-stage screen: it selects the winner among the two finalists and does not rank all twelve configurations at full budget. C$^*$ is the winner of this screen under the development objective; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule, and R3 embodies earlier development choices that the comparison does not equalise. C$^*$/S0 is C$^*$ with B3's spread pacing.

\paragraph{The repair R4 and tuned variants.} R4 is R3 with the terminal reserve capped at $\min(JL, \lfloor A/2 \rfloor)$, and C$^*$/S2 is C$^*$ with that schedule. They differ from R3 and C$^*$ only when $JL > \lfloor A/2 \rfloor$: they equal them in the second test's blocks at $\rho = \const{0.2}$ and appear only in its repair block at $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's reserve consumes the whole allowance. Separately tuned versions of B3, R3, and C$^*$ (B3-t, R3-t, C$^*$-t), each over a common grid of \const{16} tolerance and ban-length pairs and so possibly at other pairs than the fixed one, are a secondary comparison.
\paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are \site{r_q2_p} for the priority phase P and \site{r_q2_g} for newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and \site{r_q2_s} for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: \site{r_q2_s_x}). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G's conditional effects, is \site{r_q2_pg}; G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase and by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it, and the priority phase changes it by only \site{r_q2_p_g1} once G is in place (averaged over both pacing settings). R3's own comparison, $F_{111}$ minus $F_{011}$ (exploratory, from the evaluation records), is \site{r_dp} pooled: exactly zero on four markets, where both rules leave the attacker the same gain, and nonzero on fresh1 (\site{r_dp_f1}) and fresh3. Against the fixed attacks, the priority phase's effect given newest-first targeting is larger (\site{r_q2_fixed_pg1} under D2+H3 and \site{r_q2_fixed_pg1_x} under X7-SC, averaged over pacing), though still small next to newest-first targeting's effect without the priority phase (above). In the pooled, separately searched comparisons, given newest-first targeting the priority phase adds little at these audit ratios and capacities; at a lower audit ratio or a larger attacker capacity, half of a round's audits may no longer cover one provider's jobs, and the substitution need not hold. In the pooled contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight selected attacks (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed as scored (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}); the stricter eligibility reading of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} leaves them not assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2-x7sc}).
\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. \site{r_flag} of the \site{r_flag_n} doubling checks are flagged, and fresh-market factorial cells outside Q1 and Q4 have no doubling check, so their narrow intervals describe seed noise only. A nesting comparison on the existing records (exploratory) shows evaluation discrepancies that call for further search checks, also in unflagged cells: X7-SC contains D2+H3, yet in \site{r_nest_over} of \site{r_nest_n} cells the X7-SC winner's evaluated gain falls short of the better of the frozen and doubled D2+H3 winners on the same seeds by more than the doubling threshold (at most \site{r_nest_max}). Winners are selected on training seeds and need not keep their order on evaluation seeds, and picking the better D2+H3 winner on the evaluation seeds favours it, so this is a discrepancy, not a demonstrated search failure. The report lists the search-flagged cells, and the addendum traces the inherited flags of every Q1 and Q4 decision through the records' references. Q2 and Q3 statements are descriptive; the cells they use carry the flags listed in the report. Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability} sets the Q1 estimates beside their re-evaluation with the registered configurations, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} compares the Q4 estimates, calibrated lower bounds, and decisions under both configurations and gives the adjusted market $p$-value under the registered configuration; the largest Q4 change is on fresh4, where the gain difference moves from \site{r_st_f4_old} to \site{r_st_f4_new}, still above the registered \const{0.10}.

