Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:
Two Registered Tests of an Audit Rule
Ömer Demirel
Draft 9, November 2026

Abstract
A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider
can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery,
costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that for the
uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing,
because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals
whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a
new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. A registered
test compares it with a verified-history comparator, C∗ , at matched budget and sanctions on
six markets drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash. Four findings. First, pooled over
the six markets, R3 and C∗ are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” within a pre-set margin of
±0.05 (C∗ minus R3 under D2+H3 attacks: 0.021); exploratory t intervals over the six market
differences are [−0.013, 0.054] with the frozen searches, [0.014, 0.045] with the doubled searches,
and [0.009, 0.052] with each rule’s strongest family, so the direction is fairly stable but the
size, and with it the equivalence label, is fragile and not established beyond these markets;
the second attack family repeated the first family’s winner in 10 of 12 cells. Second, newestfirst targeting does most of the work: in R3’s own comparison (with and without the priority
window, under D2+H3), the window changes the attacker’s gain by −0.003, small next to its
effect without newest-first targeting. Third, at the headline market the targeting also falls on
an honest newcomer: R3 audits 0.917 of a replacement identity’s jobs in its second ten rounds,
C∗ 0.161 in the same bin, and the replacement’s profit per round under R3 differs from that
under C∗ by a fraction of −0.044. Fourth, under D2+H3, R3 lowers the attacker’s gain relative
to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on 3 of six; at
the headline market, welfare attacks lower all three rules’ gross efficiency to about 0.840 while
earning less than the truthful reference against R3 (gain −0.094). The claims hold for the
prescribed searches and frozen references, the evaluated markets, fixed parameters, the searched
families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities, so honest churn is untested;
C∗ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.

1

Introduction

A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost
and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are
expensive and run after the response is served. FlashProve, for example, proves that a committed
integer model produced a given token under greedy decoding; the provider generates proofs after the
response is served, at about 70 to 95 times the cost of one forward pass [27, p. 18], for a window of
1024 tokens per request, and the client checks each proof on a CPU [27, pp. 16–17]. Its Lean proofs
cover the protocol; the equivalence of the executable prover and verifier to it is not proved [27, p. 17].
1

Such proofs detect a substituted computation; they do not prevent an incorrect response from being
served. If the checking party funds such checks, a budget forces a sample, and the questions become
which jobs to check and how to sanction a provider whose check fails.
We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identities are
unavailable. The mechanism class excludes entry fees, identity certification, and collateral before
receipts, and a sanctioned provider may re-enter at once under a fresh identity. The rules refund the
escrow of a failed job and exclude the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. The population
is a fixed set of providers, each with one active identity at a time; concurrent aliases, pre-aged
reserve identities, and additional entrants (Sybil attacks [10]) are outside the model.
Checking newcomers is an old idea. Intensive verification until an identity has earned credibility is established: credibility-based spot-checking analyses rejoining under new identities [28, 29];
BOINC offers an optional adaptive-replication policy that replicates a host’s jobs until the host
passes a trust threshold of consecutive valid results [2, 1]; customs guidance recommends checking
all declarations of new importers for a period [35]. Checking can also depend on what a participant
reports [40]; our admissible rules may read offers and claims, but we study targeting by identity age
and verified history. The question we ask is narrower: under a hard audit budget, does R3’s persistent age-ranked targeting do better or worse than targeting by verified history, when everything
else is held equal?
The rule. For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals
whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation,
immediate free replacement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to
identity relabelling, so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary 1).
R3 gives audit priority to the jobs of identities in their first L rounds, auditing all of them when
the round’s quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established
identities. Its priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement
is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least min(s, ⌈r/2⌉) of the r residual audits,
s being its served jobs (Lemma 2 and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a
replacement receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer
entrants, or a quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity’s first offer.
Two registered tests. Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped
before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments.
The first test (October 2026) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results
then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second
test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their
seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition,
stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test’s comparison.
Contributions. Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker
gain within the pre-set materiality margin of ±0.05; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first,
and the classification is established for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3’s priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other:
either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker’s gain, so given newest-first targeting
the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) In a registered diagnostic, R3 keeps auditing an honest
2

provider’s replacement identity well after its window, at a measured cost in that provider’s profit per
round: the targeting that works against the attacker also burdens honest newcomers. Supporting
these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure
boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse
and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market
replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
Results in brief. Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C∗ are “equivalent, direction R3
lower” within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3 (the two families are
close to one test, since X7-SC mostly selected the embedded D2+H3 winner): R3 is not materially
better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget, and the difference varies in sign across markets. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and
newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects −0.083 and −0.095), and either
one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower
measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison
that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack
lowers R3’s gross efficiency to 0.840, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the
evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the 0.997 of the registered W1 search at non-negative
estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency
bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures’ population profitability is not established. Q4:
the B3–R3 comparison passes on 3 of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on
the other three, R3’s estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.

2

Related work

Verification-based probation. Credibility-based spot-checking accepts results according to a
worker’s verified history and analyses rejoining under new identities [28, 29]; follow-on work treats
imperfect checks [36]. BOINC’s adaptive replication keeps a consecutive-valid count per host and
trusts a host above a threshold [2, 1]. CrowdEval screens workers with gold tasks before balancing
gold and peer evaluation [26]. Customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new
importers for a period [35], and a field experiment found no overall compliance gain from close
supervision of new firms [14]. In a model with adverse selection and moral hazard, optimal longterm contracts screen and then put high types on probation with monitoring that declines with the
track record [5]. Our comparator family adapts the credibility and BOINC rules to a fixed audit
quota (Section 4).
Cheap pseudonyms. With cheap pseudonyms, unfavourable treatment of newcomers is the price
of cooperation [13]; whitewashing degrades peer-to-peer systems when turnover is high [11]; without
a central authority one attacker can create many identities [10]. Reputation mechanisms with pure
moral hazard and the cost of changing identities are analysed in [9]. Reputation without persistent
identity in markets of AI agents, with a one-time cash-out deviation, is studied in [15]; that model
has no verification budget.
Costly verification and audits. Costly state verification goes back to [31]; history-dependent
audit groups under restricted penalties go back to [18]; allocation with costly verification [3], limited punishments [20], financially constrained agents [21], and ex post verification with limited

