B

Proofs

Lemma 1. Shading saves sg. Each audited escrow is refunded with probability d instead of d0 .
Given K, independence of delivery noise gives the probabilities 1 − (1 − d)K and 1 − (1 − d0 )K of
at least one failure; the continuation loss is incurred once. Taking expectations over U gives the
identity.
Corollary 1. Let Vclean and Vfail be the best continuation values after no focal failure and after
at least one, so CU = Vclean − Vfail . By the first condition, immediate free replacement attains the
clean distribution of future states after a failure, so Vfail ≥ Vclean . By the second, any plan after
a failure (keeping the banned identity and waiting, or replacing at any later time) can be copied
after no failure, so Vfail ≤ Vclean . Hence CU = 0. For B2 and B3 the second condition holds because
a failed identity carries only its ban, with no priority or payment privilege, a clean provider can
copy waiting by not offering and can itself replace its identity, no bond is lost, and the audit budget
and rival states do not depend on the focal identity’s history; replacement restores eligibility before
the next allocation. Refunds of the failed jobs in the current round still enter Lemma 1. A rule
that gave priority to an identity that waits out its ban would violate the second condition, and the
corollary would not apply to it.
Lemma 2. R3’s priority list is ordered by age group, newest first. Within a group, the audit
keys are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables, so the group’s jobs
are exchangeable and the selected subset is uniform among them; delivery noise is independent of
selection.

